{
  "requirement": "TBV owns the model conversation, supported backend integrations, tool\ndispatch, and structured submission used by its ask builder.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The subject `requirements.sdk.graph.agent-harness.tool_agent` is an umbrella obligation. TBV owns four things used by the ask builder: the model conversation, the supported backend integrations, tool dispatch, and structured submission. The finer points are delegated to refining requirements: `submission.*`, `tool_agent.isolation`, `tool_agent.provider_failure`, `fixed_tools` and `agent_config.*`. Those carry their own tests, so I did not reassess them here.\n\nMapping the four obligations to the marked tests:\n\n- **Model conversation (history, multi-turn, tool results fed back):**\n  - `agent/tests.rs#fn[application_tools_preserve_graph_tools_in_the_same_conversation]` asserts what each turn delivers. It checks that every request carries all tools, that tool-result messages contain the expected payloads, and that paging works across turns.\n  - `agent/tests.rs#fn[concurrent_tool_agents_isolate_histories_pages_and_submissions_and_share_reads]` checks that two concurrent conversations stay separate. It asserts each makes 3 calls and that the recorded dependencies match.\n  - `agent/tests.rs#fn[agent_executions_isolate_conversations_and_dependency_traces]` runs two concurrent verifications and asserts per-call outputs, call counts and dependency traces.\n- **Supported backend integrations:**\n  - OpenAI: `configured.rs#mod[tests]/fn[supplied_openai_backend_runs_independent_calls_against_a_local_transport]` drives a real local HTTP transport. It asserts the endpoint path, model, token limit, tool list and order, and the `submit_result` schema. It also asserts that the tool output is replayed on the next turn and that secrets are absent from persisted evidence.\n  - Ollama: `configured.rs#mod[tests]/fn[supplied_ollama_backend_delivers_tools_and_results_over_native_chat]` covers the native chat path, bearer auth, options, the tool schema, and delivery of the tool result in the second turn.\n  - Bedrock: `providers.rs#mod[tests]` has three marked tests. They check option typing and the `max_tokens` default, cache checkpoint placement, and order-preserving normalization of typed client calls with native history. A live Bedrock round trip would need AWS services, and the other Bedrock setup and credential tests in `providers.rs` cover the transport construction. I treat the end-to-end Bedrock call as a legitimate exemption.\n- **Tool dispatch:**\n  - `agent/tests.rs#fn[application_tool_factories_create_fresh_state_for_each_agent_call]` checks that application tools run, that fresh per-call state is created, and that results are fed back with the right counters.\n  - `agent/tests.rs#fn[source_tools_keep_search_discovery_separate_from_delivered_source_reads]` dispatches `search_text` and `read_source`. It asserts that only the delivered read becomes a dependency.\n  - `application_tools_preserve_graph_tools_in_the_same_conversation` dispatches the graph tools `outgoing_edges`, `read_node` and `get_next_page` alongside the application tool `increment`.\n- **Structured submission:**\n  - The OpenAI and Ollama tests assert that `submit_result` is exposed with a `required: [\"result\"]` schema. They also assert that the submitted value is accepted as the typed answer.\n  - `agent/tests.rs#fn[behavioral_adequacy_uses_domain_contents_and_returns_a_typed_fingerprinted_answer]` asserts that a structured result is returned, typed and stored. It also checks the system prompt and the schema of the submitted type.\n\nI found no plausible requirement-violating behavior that these tests would accept. I did not need implementation source.",
  "references": "152e27f9c9e7bc9c3e830b7fbfb7088f5da2327804c01b46add84e64cd3e0136",
